.TH EDIT 2
.SH NAME
put,
erase_back,
text,
cursor,
clear \- a line under construction
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libedit"
.PP
.nf
.B "Line :: struct { buf: []u8, n: int, pos: int }"
.B "Result :: enum { Edited, Done, Full, Pending }"
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B "put :: proc(l: ^Line, b: u8) -> Result"
.B "erase_back :: proc(line: []u8, key: u8) -> int"
.B "text :: proc(l: ^Line) -> string"
.B "cursor :: proc(l: ^Line) -> int"
.B "clear :: proc(l: ^Line)"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Libedit
is one line discipline, the keys it acts on and what they do, shared by
every program in ring 3 that holds a line of typed text: a window's
.B cons
in
.IR intuition (4),
and
.I window
and
.I terminal
(see
.IR window (1))
over their own raw keyboard.
It is
.IR rio 's
.B wbswidth
as a package.
.PP
A
.B Line
is a fixed buffer, how much of it holds text, and a cursor position.
The storage is the caller's:
.I put
never grows it.
.I Pos
is always between zero and
.IR n .
.PP
.I Put
gives one byte to
.I l
and answers what it did.
.RB ( ".Edited" )
covers both an inserted character and an erased one, because both mean
the same thing to a caller that echoes: draw the line again.
.RB ( ".Done" )
is a newline: the line is ready, and the newline itself is not stored,
because a caller that wants one appends it and a caller that draws the
line does not.
.RB ( ".Full" )
is a character that did not fit.
.RB ( ".Pending" )
is a byte that changed nothing, including a byte still arriving as part
of a multi-byte rune (see
.IR keyboard (6))
and a rune this discipline has no use for.
.PP
.I Put
acts on these keys:
.TP 12
.BR \eb " or " DEL
erase one character before the cursor
.TP
.B ^U
erase the whole line
.TP
.B ^W
erase one word: skip any characters before the cursor that are not
letters or digits, then erase the run of letters and digits behind them
.TP
.BR ^A " or " Khome
move the cursor to the beginning of the line
.TP
.BR ^E " or " Kend
move the cursor to the end of the line
.TP
.BR Kleft ", " Kright
move the cursor one character
.PD
.PP
.BR Khome ,
.BR Kend ,
.B Kleft
and
.B Kright
are the rune names
.IR keyboard (6)
gives the arrow and home and end keys.
Every other character is inserted
.I at
the cursor, and every other rune, including one this discipline has no
key binding for, changes nothing: this package holds only 7-bit ASCII,
because
.IR font (2)
has no glyph for anything else.
.PP
.I Erase_back
answers how many bytes one erase key removes from the end of
.IR line ,
which is
.IR put 's
rule for
.B \eb
.RB ( DEL )
and
.BR ^W ,
exposed for a caller that wants it directly.
It answers zero for an empty line and for a byte that is not an erase
key.
.PP
.I Text
is the line typed so far.
.I Cursor
is where the next character goes, as an index into
.IR text .
.I Clear
empties the line and its cursor, and drops any rune still arriving.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libedit/edit.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR keyboard (6),
.IR kbd (2),
.IR font (2),
.IR window (1)
.SH BUGS
Only ASCII is stored.
A rune above
.B 0x7F
that is not one of the four motion keys is silently dropped rather than
inserted or reported.
